The proposed listing of Jio Platforms could prove to be a major catalyst for a re-rating of Reliance Industries (RIL) , by giving the conglomerate’s largest growth asset an independent market valuation and bringing greater transparency to its sum-of-the-parts (SOTP) structure, according to a research report by Religare Broking.
The brokerage has assigned a BUY rating to RIL, with a target price of Rs 1,696, implying an upside of 46.7% from the stock’s closing price of Rs 1,156 on October 12. Currently, the stock trades at Rs 1,175.
At the heart of Religare’s investment thesis is the potential for Jio’s listing to narrow the holding-company discount at which conglomerates typically trade. The lack of transparency in valuing individual businesses and ambiguity around internal capital allocation often weigh on conglomerate valuations. An independent, publicly quoted price for Jio would make RIL’s largest growth asset easier to value and provide greater clarity on the group’s SOTP valuation.
This, the brokerage argues, could set off a chain of events: independent price discovery for Jio, greater transparency in RIL’s SOTP valuation, compression of the conglomerate discount and, eventually, a re-rating of the parent company.
Religare believes investors should accumulate RIL shares ahead of the proposed listing rather than wait for Jio’s market debut. Institutional equity markets tend to price in major corporate catalysts before their formal execution, and waiting until the listing could mean missing the initial phase of multiple expansion. Investing in RIL at current levels would allow investors to participate in Jio’s value discovery while retaining exposure to the group’s other businesses — Reliance Retail, oil-to-chemicals (O2C) and new energy.
The brokerage also sees the stock’s recent price performance as favourable for entry. RIL has undergone an extended two-year price and time correction, which, in Religare’s assessment, has removed the valuation premium previously attached to future growth expectations. The correction has also allowed baseline operating earnings across consumer platforms to catch up with share prices. With the stock trading near the lower boundary of its multi-year range, the brokerage sees downside risk as well-protected, with the proposed Jio listing providing upside potential.
 Jio’s transition from network rollout to monetisation 
Jio’s operating performance and improving cash flow profile form another important part of the investment case. The business has moved from a capital-intensive telecom rollout phase towards monetising its large subscriber base through higher average revenue per user (ARPU), 5G premiumisation and digital services.
With a substantial part of its 5G network capital expenditure largely complete, Jio’s capital intensity is expected to moderate. Religare identifies a steady ARPU trajectory, migration from 4G to 5G premium plans, increasing data throughput and the expansion of enterprise cloud and artificial intelligence services as the key drivers of earnings growth.
Jio reported revenue from operations of Rs 1,46,885 crore in FY26, while EBITDA stood at Rs 76,255 crore, translating into a margin of 51.9%. Net profit was Rs 30,049 crore, and its active subscriber base exceeded 524 million.
The proposed IPO could also help improve Jio’s balance sheet. According to the draft red herring prospectus, up to Rs 27,500 crore of net proceeds from the primary issue is earmarked for prepayment or repayment of outstanding borrowings at Reliance Jio Infocomm Ltd.
The repayment of debt would reduce interest expenses, expand net profit margins and accelerate free cash flow conversion. A stronger balance sheet and higher cash accruals at Jio would further strengthen the financial position of parent RIL, the brokerage said.
 Retail, O2C and new energy offer additional growth levers 
While Jio is the immediate catalyst for the proposed re-rating, Religare’s investment case also rests on the contribution of the group’s other businesses.
Reliance Retail’s growth is expected to be driven by expansion of its store network and the scaling up of digital commerce. The brokerage sees the potential for operating leverage to improve as the business expands, alongside the possibility of a future listing that could provide another avenue for value discovery.
