Whether the rally seen in the previous session can sustain itself will be key to watch in the coming sessions. Although the broader market structure remains weak, momentum indicators have shown signs of strength. If the Nifty 50 manages to defend its immediate support at 22,600, its 10-day (EMA), it could move towards 22,800 and then 23,000, despite the possibility of some consolidation. However, a break below 22,600 could expose the index to further downside, with the 22,300–22,200 zone acting as a crucial support area. Meanwhile, the Bank Nifty needs to hold support at 55,400 and 55,100 to avoid potential selling pressure. On the upside, resistance is placed at 56,100 and 56,500, experts said.
On October 13, the Nifty 50 rallied 359 points, or 1.6 percent, to close at 22,722, while the Bank Nifty gained 563 points, or 1.03 percent, to settle at 55,481. Market breadth on the National Stock Exchange (NSE) favoured the bulls, with 1,903 stocks advancing compared with 1,364 declining.
 Ashish Kyal, CMT, Founder and CEO of Waves Strategy Advisors 
Buying interest returned following the recent correction, supported by strength in global markets. A key trigger for the afternoon rally was Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman’s comments on Long-Term Capital Gains (LTCG) tax and Securities Transaction Tax (STT), which raised hopes of a possible review. Adding to the positive momentum, the IT sector surged over 5%, driven by strong quarterly results from HCLTech and TCS, making it one of the key contributors to the index’s recovery.
On the daily chart, the ongoing pullback is expected to continue in the form of Wave F of a Diametric pattern. After four consecutive sessions, the Nifty finally closed above the previous day’s high, indicating a temporary pause in the ongoing downtrend. On shorter time frames, prices have broken above the key Point of Control (POC) area on the volume profile, which is a positive sign. Based on this, support is placed near 22,620 in Nifty Futures, which roughly corresponds to 22,480 on the spot index.
The Nifty is showing signs of a recovery from lower levels. For now, the trend has shifted towards a buy-on-dips approach, with an upside target of 22,820, as long as 22,480 holds on the downside.
 Strategy : Buy Nifty Futures if prices move towards 22,630–22,650 and reverse higher. Maintain a stop-loss at 22,520, with targets of 22,730–22,760, followed by 22,820.
