Department of Investment and Public Asset Management (DIPAM) Secretary Arunish Chawla said, "The government is divesting 30.48% stake in IDBI Bank, while LIC is divesting 30.24%." Canada Inc will pay Rs 81 per share to buy the stake in IDBI Bank. The price is at a 2.58% discount to Tuesday's closing price. The IDBI Bank stocks surged 8.33 percent to Rs 89.85 on Wednesday. The announcement was made after market hours.
Union Minister Ashwini Vaishnaw said the government and LIC would retain a combined 34% stake in the restructured entity . "This will attract the largest FDI in the financial sector," he said.
As per the share purchase agreement, the invested capital will be locked in for three years, and IDBI Bank will retain its existing brand name. LIC will have to reduce its stake in the bank to 15% within two years, in line with Reserve Bank of India norms.
Chawla said, "As per RBI norms, Canada Inc will have to decide within two years of execution of the share purchase agreement whether it will merge CSB Bank with IDBI Bank or sell its stake in CSB Bank."
The share purchase agreement is expected to be signed and executed before the end of the current calendar year, Chawla said.
"The benchmark price for evaluation of qualified bids was Rs 75.99 per share. The winning bid was higher than the benchmark," the DIPAM secretary added.
Canada Inc will now present its credentials to the RBI for a 'fit and proper' assessment and submit a roadmap for compliance with regulatory conditions.
All RBI and SEBI related norms will be followed, Vaishnaw on question of whether Strategic Buyer will be allowed to have holding in two banks.
