The Cabinet's approval of IDBI Bank's strategic restructuring could help the Centre offset some of the fiscal slippage risks arising from higher fertiliser subsidies and excise duty cuts on petrol and diesel, with economists expecting disinvestment and asset monetisation receipts to exceed the Budget target for the first time in eight years.
The government could raise between Rs 90,000 crore and Rs 1 lakh crore from disinvestment and asset monetisation in 2026-27, against the Budget estimate of Rs 80,000 crore, according to Gaura Sengupta, chief economist at IDFC FIRST Bank.
"It's definitely positive news. Assuming it's completed in FY27, then total disinvestment receipts, including monetisation, exceed the target of Rs 80,000 crore. Total collection could reach Rs 90,000 crore to Rs 1 lakh crore by March," Sengupta said.
The additional receipts would provide some cushion against expenditure and revenue pressures, although the fiscal deficit could still exceed the government's target.
"If expenditure and other receipts remain unchanged, the Rs 8,000 crore overshoot would therefore lower the fiscal deficit and financing requirement by this amount. Against the budgeted FY27 deficit of ₹16.96 lakh crore, or 4.3% of GDP, this represents roughly 0.5% of the deficit and 0.02 percentage point of GDP—insufficient, by itself, to drastically alter the headline ratio," Manoranjan Sharma, chief economist, Infomerics Ratings said.
