"This will counter some of the fiscal slippage risk facing the Centre due to higher fertiliser subsidy cost as well as excise duty cuts on petrol and diesel. Overall fiscal deficit is expected to exceed target levels," Sengupta added.
Madan Sabnavis, chief economist at Bank of Baroda, said the approval was expected and would help strengthen the disinvestment programme, which has already performed well this financial year.
"Disinvestment this year has been quite successful and with sale of IDBI there will be further support. At a time when expenditure can overshoot on subsidy this component will provide some compensation," said Sabnavis.
The Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs (CCEA) on October 14 approved the strategic restructuring of IDBI Bank, clearing the sale of a 60.72 percent stake to Fairfax Financial Holdings-backed 10955230 Canada Inc. for Rs 52,878 crore.
Of this, around Rs 26,500 crore will accrue to the Centre from the sale of its 30.48 percent stake, while the remaining proceeds will go to Life Insurance Corporation of India (LIC), which is selling a 30.24 percent holding.
