The Centre had already mobilised Rs 62,124 crore from disinvestment and asset monetisation as of September 10, accounting for nearly 78 percent of its full-year target.
Of this, Rs 55,757 crore came from disinvestment and another Rs 6,367 crore from asset monetisation, according to data from the Department of Investment and Public Asset Management (DIPAM).
"The IDBI stake sale could help the Government of India overshoot its FY2027 target for miscellaneous capital receipts, thereby providing some elbow room on the fiscal front amidst the fiscal challenges posed by the West Asia crisis," said Aditi Nayar, chief economist, Icra.
The IDBI transaction could take combined receipts to around Rs 88,700 crore, nearly 11 percent above the original Budget estimate, provided the deal is completed within the current financial year. Further stake sales and asset monetisation could lift receipts closer to the Rs 1 lakh crore level projected by economists.
Official sources have indicated that the IDBI transaction is expected to be completed within FY27, with the share purchase agreement expected to be signed before the end of calendar year 2026.
If receipts exceed the Budget estimate, it would mark the first time since 2018-19 that the Centre has surpassed its original target.
In FY19, disinvestment receipts stood at Rs 84,972 crore against a Budget estimate of Rs 80,000 crore. Since then, receipts have consistently fallen short of the amounts projected at the beginning of the financial year.
